1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,974
You have a spare disk, and you want to use it with LVM, the Logical Volume Manager.

2
00:00:05,994 --> 00:00:11,367
Before LVM can touch the disk, the disk has to be prepared as a physical volume.

3
00:00:11,837 --> 00:00:14,974
In this video: create and remove physical volumes.

4
00:00:19,494 --> 00:00:22,077
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:22,547 --> 00:00:24,745
Create and remove physical volumes.

6
00:00:25,215 --> 00:00:29,367
We'll check there are none, create one on a blank disk, look at its details,

7
00:00:29,387 --> 00:00:30,465
and remove it again.

8
00:00:39,985 --> 00:00:44,653
LVM builds storage in three layers. At the bottom, physical volumes:

9
00:00:44,673 --> 00:00:48,031
disks, or partitions, that LVM is allowed to use.

10
00:00:48,051 --> 00:00:52,237
On top of them, volume groups, which pool the space together.

11
00:00:52,257 --> 00:00:57,821
And on top of those, logical volumes, which is what you finally format and mount.

12
00:01:06,291 --> 00:01:08,694
This video is only the bottom layer.

13
00:01:08,714 --> 00:01:13,539
A physical volume is a disk with a small LVM label written at its start.

14
00:01:13,559 --> 00:01:15,423
The next videos build on it.

15
00:01:23,893 --> 00:01:28,256
We use the empty disk vdb. As always, check the disk name first:

16
00:01:28,276 --> 00:01:30,380
these commands change the disk.

17
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:43,389
First, pvs, with sudo. It lists physical volumes. Right now it prints nothing:

18
00:01:43,409 --> 00:01:46,285
this machine has no physical volumes yet.

19
00:01:53,805 --> 00:01:59,295
Now pvcreate, and the disk. It says: physical volume successfully created.

20
00:01:59,315 --> 00:02:05,326
The second line is just LVM creating its devices file, the first time it is used.

21
00:02:12,796 --> 00:02:19,395
pvs again. Now there is one line. The name is vdb. The volume group column is empty:

22
00:02:19,415 --> 00:02:22,468
nobody uses it yet. The format is lvm2.

23
00:02:22,488 --> 00:02:26,408
The size is five gigabytes, and all of it is free.

24
00:02:36,928 --> 00:02:40,444
pvdisplay shows the details of one physical volume.

25
00:02:40,464 --> 00:02:42,940
The name, the size, and a unique ID.

26
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:48,695
Allocatable says no, and the extents are zero, because no volume group uses it yet.

27
00:02:48,715 --> 00:02:51,329
We will see these numbers change soon.

28
00:03:02,849 --> 00:03:06,285
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

29
00:03:07,805 --> 00:03:11,216
We reboot. Then pvs shows the physical volume again:

30
00:03:11,236 --> 00:03:14,910
the label is written on the disk itself, so it survives.

31
00:03:24,430 --> 00:03:29,187
Now we remove it. pvremove, and the disk. It wipes the LVM label.

32
00:03:29,207 --> 00:03:33,818
This only works while no volume group uses the physical volume.

33
00:03:42,288 --> 00:03:47,306
pvs prints nothing again, and lsblk still shows the disk, untouched,

34
00:03:47,326 --> 00:03:49,159
ready for something else.

35
00:03:58,679 --> 00:04:04,215
Your turn. Create a physical volume on the disk vdc. Remove the one on vdb.

36
00:04:04,235 --> 00:04:05,400
Run the checker.

37
00:04:11,870 --> 00:04:13,621
Try before you open the solution.

38
00:04:19,141 --> 00:04:22,278
Next up: assign physical volumes to volume groups.

39
00:04:22,748 --> 00:04:25,651
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
